# Generated TypeScript artifacts. Both were tracked, and with several
# terminals open on this repo they were the main source of spurious diffs —
# they get rewritten by every `next dev` and `next build`, so they turn up
# dirty constantly and get swept into commits by a `git add -A` that meant to
# catch something else. That already produced a commit whose message described
# work living in a different commit.
#
# next-env.d.ts: Next's own docs are explicit — "managed by Next.js... Add it
# to .gitignore. If your project already tracks the file, remove it from Git."
# It stays in tsconfig.json's `include` array and is regenerated by next dev /
# next build / next typegen, so ignoring it costs nothing. It also flips its
# routes import between ./.next/dev/types and ./.next/types depending on which
# of those ran last, which is most of the churn.
#
# tsconfig.tsbuildinfo: TypeScript's incremental cache (compilerOptions
# .incremental is true), 731KB, regenerated on demand and read by nothing in
# this repo.
next-env.d.ts
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# Environment files
#
# NOTE the backup patterns below. `.env*.local` matches `.env.local` but NOT
# `.env.local.bak`, `.env.local.2026-08-10` or anything else appended AFTER
# the `.local` suffix — and making a timestamped copy before editing an env
# file is exactly what anyone does when they are being careful. That copy
# carries every secret in the original and lands in `git status` as untracked,
# one `git add -A` away from being committed. It has already happened once in
# this repo, caught only because someone ran `git check-ignore` on it.
.env
.env.local
.env*.bak
.env*.bak-*
.env.local.*
*.env.backup
.env.development.local
.env.test.local
.env.production.local

# Debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# Misc
.DS_Store
*.pem
.vercel
# Markdown docs (all .md files excluded from repo)
*.md
# ...except the project instructions. These are rules for anyone working in this
# repo — the branch policy exists to stop commits landing on main — and a rule
# that lives only on one laptop protects only that laptop.
!CLAUDE.md
# ...and the skills, for the same reason. A skill is a checklist that runs
# before a page ships; one that exists only on the machine it was written on
# catches nothing anywhere else. Narrow on purpose — this re-includes skill
# definitions, not documentation generally.
!.claude/skills/**/*.md
# ...and agent definitions, for exactly the same reason. An agent that owns a
# workflow — what it runs, what it must never do, what it already learned — is
# worth nothing if it exists only on the laptop it was written on. The rules in
# one of these files are the difference between an agent that repeats a mistake
# and one that does not.
!.claude/agents/**/*.md
# ...and research plans under docs/. These carry the reasoning behind what got
# built and — more usefully — what got REJECTED and why, which is the part no
# commit message captures. The California plan records that the exam-language
# pass-rate gap has zero search demand; without that written down, the next
# person re-derives it and builds the page anyway.
# Named explicitly rather than !docs/*.md — that pattern would have swept in 17
# pre-existing design docs that were deliberately untracked, as a side effect of
# committing one research plan.
!docs/california-content-plan.md
# ...and the Data Reels Agent manual. This one is the operating instructions for
# a pipeline that publishes to the public internet on a schedule with nobody
# watching. If it lives only on one laptop, the first person to inherit it has
# a cron they cannot explain and a set of rules — no unsourced figures, no
# personal data, no naming a business — that exist nowhere they will look.
!docs/data-reels-agent.md
# ...and the JSON-LD reference, for the same reason and one more. It is the only
# written record of WHY two deprecated types are deliberately kept, and it holds
# dated claims about Google's behaviour that go stale silently — the FAQ
# citation in it had already become a 301 without anyone noticing, because a
# redirect resolves and a 404 doesn't. Untracked, corrections to it live on one
# laptop and the next person re-derives them from scratch.
!jsonld.md
# ...and the Gaming agent manual. Same reason as the Data Reels one: it is the
# operating record for something published to the public internet, and its rules
# are the difference between a game that teaches the board's requirements and
# one that teaches an invented requirement to somebody about to sit the exam.
# The rule that no wrong answer may ever be invented, the three sourced
# distractor classes, and the two bugs that passed every test — none of that is
# recoverable from the code, and untracked it lives on one laptop.
!docs/gaming-agent.md



